edu//cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/LATN/W3998-20211-020/"},{"code":"LATN UN3998","title":"SUPERVSD RESRCH IN LATIN LIT","subtitle":"SUPERVSD RESRCH IN LATIN","desc":"Prerequisites: the director of undergraduate studies permission. A program of research in Latin literature. Research paper required.","dept":"Classics @Barnard","deptCode":"LATN","instructor":"Ellen Morris","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[],"start":null,"end":null,"startLabel":null,"endLabel":null,"location":null,"campus":"Barnard College","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"00674","enrolled":0,"capacity":3,"enrollSeries":[["2021-06-16",0]],"enrollLastDate":"2021-06-16","classId":"W3998-20211-021","openTo":[],"type":"INDEPENDENT","link":"http://www.columbia.edu//cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/LATN/W3998-20211-021/"},{"code":"LING GU4120","title":"LANG DOCUMENTATION/FIELD MTHDS","subtitle":"LANG DOCUMENTATION/FIELD","desc":"Prerequisites: LING UN3101 In light of the predicted loss of up to 90% of the world languages by the end of this century, it has become urgent that linguists take a more active role in documenting and conserving endangered languages. In this course, we will learn the essential skills and technology of language documentation through work with speakers of an endangered language.","dept":"Slavic Languages","deptCode":"LING","instructor":"Meredith Landman","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[1,3],"start":880,"end":955,"startLabel":"2:40pm","endLabel":"3:55pm","location":"ONLINE ONLY","campus":"Morningside","method":"On-Line Only","points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[["LING UN3101"]],"callNumber":"10074","enrolled":5,"capacity":12,"enrollSeries":[["2021-06-16",5]],"enrollLastDate":"2021-06-16","classId":"W4120-20211-001","openTo":[],"type":"WORKSHOP","link":"http://www.columbia.edu//cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/LING/W4120-20211-001/"},{"code":"LING GU4173","title":"Hyphenated Minds: Heritage Speakers and","subtitle":"Hyphenated Minds: Heritag","desc":"This graduate-undergraduate humanities laboratory is designed to evoke interest in the mental lexicon of heritage speakers, a conspicuous gap in heritage language studies. While most examples that the lecturer will provide will be from the heritage speakers of Slavic languages, the course will be open to graduate students in all foreign languages. The class-contact part will first explore the key concepts in mental lexicon and bilingualism. Then, in the real-life lab part, the students would visit the areas in the City with a high concentration of heritage speakers (such as Brighton Beach for those concentrating on Russian or Astoria for those with interest in BCS). They would then conduct research and report on it in the class. Their research would be critiqued in class discussions and the students would be pointed to the directions of further research.","dept":"Slavic Languages","deptCode":"LING","instructor":null,"culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[2],"start":970,"end":1080,"startLabel":"4:10pm","endLabel":"6:00pm","location":"ONLINE ONLY","campus":"Morningside","method":"On-Line Only","points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"10775","enrolled":14,"capacity":25,"enrollSeries":[["2021-06-16",14]],"enrollLastDate":"2021-06-16","classId":"W4173-20211-001","openTo":[],"type":"SEMINAR","link":"http://www.columbia.edu//cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/LING/W4173-20211-001/"},{"code":"LING GU4376","title":"PHONETICS & PHONOLOGY","subtitle":null,"desc":"Prerequisites: LING UN3101 An investigation of the sounds of human language, from the perspective of phonetics (articulation and acoustics, including computer-aided acoustic analysis) and phonology (the distribution and function of sounds in individual languages).","dept":"Slavic Languages","deptCode":"LING","instructor":"Meredith Landman","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[2,4],"start":700,"end":775,"startLabel":"11:40am","endLabel":"12:55pm","location":"ONLINE ONLY","campus":"Morningside","method":"On-Line Only","points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[["LING UN3101"]],"callNumber":"10690","enrolled":11,"capacity":22,"enrollSeries":[["2021-06-16",11]],"enrollLastDate":"2021-06-16","classId":"W4376-20211-001","openTo":[],"type":"LECTURE","link":"http://www.columbia.edu//cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/LING/W4376-20211-001/"},{"code":"LING UN3101","title":"INTRODUCTION TO LINGUISTICS","subtitle":"INTRODUCTION TO LINGUISTI","desc":"An introduction to the study of language from a scientific perspective. The course is divided into three units: language as a system (sounds, morphology, syntax, and semantics), language in context (in space, time, and community), and language of the individual (psycholinguistics, errors, aphasia, neurology of language, and acquisition). Workload: lecture, weekly homework, and final examination.","dept":"Slavic Languages","deptCode":"LING","instructor":"John H McWhorter","culpa":null,"wikipedia":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_McWhorter","days":[2,4],"start":880,"end":955,"startLabel":"2:40pm","endLabel":"3:55pm","location":"ONLINE ONLY","campus":"Morningside","method":"On-Line Only","points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"10689","enrolled":286,"capacity":400,"enrollSeries":[["2021-06-16",286]],"enrollLastDate":"2021-06-16","classId":"W3101-20211-001","openTo":[],"type":"LECTURE","link":"http://www.columbia.edu//cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/LING/W3101-20211-001/"},{"code":"LING UN3994","title":"SUPERVISED IND RES-LING THESIS","subtitle":"SUPERVISED IND RES-LING T","desc":"","dept":"Slavic Languages","deptCode":"LING","instructor":null,"culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[],"start":null,"end":null,"startLabel":null,"endLabel":null,"location":null,"campus":"Morningside","method":"On-Line Only","points":1,"pointsLabel":"1-4","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"16607","enrolled":4,"capacity":10,"enrollSeries":[["2021-06-16",4]],"enrollLastDate":"2021-06-16","classId":"W3994-20211-001","openTo":[],"type":"WORKSHOP","link":"http://www.columbia.edu//cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/LING/W3994-20211-001/"},{"code":"MATH BC2006","title":"COMBINATORICS","subtitle":null,"desc":"","dept":"Mathematics @Barnard","deptCode":"MATH","instructor":"David A Bayer","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[2,4],"start":610,"end":685,"startLabel":"10:10am","endLabel":"11:25am","location":"To be announced","campus":"Barnard College","method":null,"points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"00086","enrolled":38,"capacity":40,"enrollSeries":[["2021-06-16",38]],"enrollLastDate":"2021-06-16","classId":"X2006-20211-001","openTo":[],"type":"LECTURE","link":"http://www.columbia.edu//cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/MATH/X2006-20211-001/"},{"code":"MATH GR5010","title":"INTRO TO THE MATH OF FINANCE","subtitle":"INTRO TO THE MATH OF FINA","desc":"Prerequisites: MATH UN1102 and MATH UN1201 , or their equivalents. Introduction to mathematical methods in pricing of options, futures and other derivative securities, risk management, portfolio management and investment strategies with an emphasis of both theoretical and practical aspects. Topics include: Arithmetic and Geometric Brownian ,motion processes, Black-Scholes partial differential equation, Black-Scholes option pricing formula, Ornstein-Uhlenbeck processes, volatility models, risk models, value-at-risk and conditional value-at-risk, portfolio construction and optimization methods.","dept":"Mathematics","deptCode":"MATH","instructor":"Mikhail Smirnov","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[1,3],"start":1180,"end":1255,"startLabel":"7:40pm","endLabel":"8:55pm","location":"312 Mathematics Building","campus":"Morningside","method":"Hybrid","points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[["MATH UN1102"],["MATH UN1201"]],"callNumber":"12273","enrolled":52,"capacity":150,"enrollSeries":[["2021-06-16",52]],"enrollLastDate":"2021-06-16","classId":"W5010-20211-001","openTo":[],"type":"LECTURE","link":"http://www.columbia.edu//cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/MATH/W5010-20211-001/"},{"code":"MATH GR5030","title":"NUMERICAL METHODS IN FINANCE","subtitle":"NUMERICAL METHODS IN FINA","desc":"Prerequisites: some familiarity with the basic principles of partial differential equations, probability and stochastic processes, and of mathematical finance as provided, e.g. in MATH W5010. Prerequisites: some familiarity with the basic principles of partial differential equations, probability and stochastic processes, and of mathematical finance as provided, e.g. in MATH W5010. Review of the basic numerical methods for partial differential equations, variational inequalities and free-boundary problems. Numerical methods for solving stochastic differential equations; random number generation, Monte Carlo techniques for evaluating path-integrals, numerical techniques for the valuation of American, path-dependent and barrier options.","dept":"Mathematics","deptCode":"MATH","instructor":"Tat Sang Fung","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[1,3],"start":1180,"end":1244,"startLabel":"7:40pm","endLabel":"8:44pm","location":"ONLINE ONLY","campus":"Morningside","method":"On-Line Only","points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[["MATH W5010"],["MATH W5010"]],"callNumber":"12272","enrolled":56,"capacity":116,"enrollSeries":[["2021-06-16",56]],"enrollLastDate":"2021-06-16","classId":"G5030-20211-001","openTo":[],"type":"LECTURE","link":"http://www.columbia.edu//cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/MATH/G5030-20211-001/"},{"code":"MATH GR5050","title":"MATH FINANCE PRACTITIONERS SEM","subtitle":"MATH FINANCE PRACTITIONER","desc":"Prerequisites: MATH W5010 or knowledge of J. Hulls book Options, futures. Prerequisites: Math GR5010 or knowledge of J. Hulls book Options, futures. Seminar consists of presentations and mini-courses by leading industry specialists in quantitative finance. Topics include portfolio optimization, exotic derivatives, high frequency analysis of data and numerical methods. While most talks require knowledge of mathematical methods in finance, some talks are accessible to general audience.","dept":"Mathematics","deptCode":"MATH","instructor":"Lars T Nielsen","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[2,4],"start":1180,"end":1255,"startLabel":"7:40pm","endLabel":"8:55pm","location":"ONLINE ONLY","campus":"Morningside","method":"On-Line Only","points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[["MATH W5010","GR5010"]],"callNumber":"12271","enrolled":55,"capacity":116,"enrollSeries":[["2021-06-16",55]],"enrollLastDate":"2021-06-16","classId":"G5050-20211-001","openTo":[],"type":"SEMINAR","link":"http://www.columbia.edu//cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/MATH/G5050-20211-001/"},{"code":"MATH GR5260","title":"PROG FOR QUANT & COMP FINANCE","subtitle":"PROG FOR QUANT & COMP FIN","desc":"This course covers programming with applications to finance. The applications may include such topics as yield curve building and calibration, short rate models, Libor market models, Monte Carlo simulation, valuation of financial instruments such as options, swaptions and variance swaps, and risk measurement and management, among others. Students will learn about the underlying theory, learn coding techniques, and get hands-on experience in implementing financial models and systems.","dept":"Mathematics","deptCode":"MATH","instructor":"Ka-Yi Ng","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[5],"start":1210,"end":1320,"startLabel":"8:10pm","endLabel":"10:00pm","location":"ONLINE ONLY","campus":"Morningside","method":"On-Line Only","points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"12270","enrolled":34,"capacity":35,"enrollSeries":[["2021-06-16",34]],"enrollLastDate":"2021-06-16","classId":"G5260-20211-001","openTo":[],"type":"LECTURE","link":"http://www.columbia.edu//cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/MATH/G5260-20211-001/"},{"code":"MATH GR5360","title":"MATH MTHDS-FIN PRICE ANALYSIS","subtitle":"MATH MTHDS-FIN PRICE ANAL","desc":"Course covers modern statistical and physical methods of analysis and prediction of financial price data. Methods from statistics, physics and econometrics will be presented with the goal to create and analyze different quantitative investment models.","dept":"Mathematics","deptCode":"MATH","instructor":"Alexei Chekhlov","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[6],"start":1140,"end":1280,"startLabel":"7:00pm","endLabel":"9:20pm","location":"ONLINE ONLY","campus":"Morningside","method":"On-Line Only","points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"12269","enrolled":26,"capacity":100,"enrollSeries":[["2021-06-16",26]],"enrollLastDate":"2021-06-16","classId":"G5360-20211-001","openTo":[],"type":"LECTURE","link":"http://www.columbia.edu//cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/MATH/G5360-20211-001/"},{"code":"MATH GR5380","title":"MULTI-ASSET PORTFOLIO MGMT","subtitle":"MULTI-ASSET PORTFOLIO MGM","desc":"The course will cover practical issues such as: how to select an investment universe and instruments, derive long term risk/return forecasts, create tactical models, construct and implement an efficient portfolio,to take into account constraints and transaction costs, measure and manage portfolio risk, and analyze the performance of the total portfolio.","dept":"Mathematics","deptCode":"MATH","instructor":null,"culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[1,3],"start":1090,"end":1165,"startLabel":"6:10pm","endLabel":"7:25pm","location":"ONLINE ONLY","campus":"Morningside","method":"On-Line Only","points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"12268","enrolled":48,"capacity":120,"enrollSeries":[["2021-06-16",48]],"enrollLastDate":"2021-06-16","classId":"G5380-20211-001","openTo":[],"type":"LECTURE","link":"http://www.columbia.edu//cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/MATH/G5380-20211-001/"},{"code":"MATH GR5400","title":"NON-LINEAR OPTION PRICING","subtitle":null,"desc":"Prerequisites: familiarity with Brownian motion, ItÃ´'s formula, stochastic differential equations, and Black-Scholes option pricing. Prerequisites: Familiarity with Brownian motion, ItÃ´'s formula, stochastic differential equations, and Black-Scholes option pricing. Nonlinear Option Pricing is a major and popular theme of research today in quantitative finance, covering a wide variety of topics such as American option pricing, uncertain volatility, uncertain mortality, different rates for borrowing and lending, calibration of models to market smiles, credit valuation adjustment (CVA), transaction costs, illiquid markets, super-replication under delta and gamma constraints, etc. The objective of this course is twofold: (1) introduce some nonlinear aspects of quantitative finance, and (2) present and compare various numerical methods for solving high-dimensional nonlinear problems arising","dept":"Mathematics","deptCode":"MATH","instructor":null,"culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[5],"start":1080,"end":1210,"startLabel":"6:00pm","endLabel":"8:10pm","location":"520 Mathematics Building","campus":"Morningside","method":"Hybrid","points":3,"pointsLabel":"3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"12267","enrolled":9,"capacity":50,"enrollSeries":[["2021-06-16",9]],"enrollLastDate":"2021-06-16","classId":"G5400-20211-001","openTo":[],"type":"LECTURE","link":"http://www.columbia.edu//cu/bulletin/uwb/subj/MATH/G5400-20211-001/"},{"code":"MATH GR5510","title":"MAFN FIELDWORK","subtitle":null,"desc":"Prerequisites: all 6 MAFN core courses, at least 6 credits of approved electives, and the instructors permission. See the MAFN website for details. This course provides an opportunity for MAFN students to engage in off-campus internships for academic credit that counts towards the degree. Graded by letter grade. Students need to secure an internship and get it approved by the instructor.","dept":"Mathematics","deptCode":"MATH","instructor":"Lars T Nielsen","culpa":null,"wikipedia":null,"days":[],"start":null,"end":null,"startLabel":null,"endLabel":null,"location":null,"campus":"Morningside","method":"On-Line Only","points":1,"pointsLabel":"1-3","prereqs":[],"callNumber":"12266","enrolled":2,"capacity":65,"enrollSeries":[["2021-06-16",2]],"enrollLastDate":"2021-06-16","classId":"G5510-20211-001","openTo":[],"type":"FIELD WORK","link":"http://www.columbia.edu//cu/bul